<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#pyidawtha">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyidawtha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1952</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pyidawtha Plan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyidawtha, meaning 'happy, prosperous land', was the welfare-state programme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a>'s government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, launched in 1952. Drawn up with American economic consultants, it planned investment in industry, agriculture, health, education and housing, financed largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> exports. Falling rice prices and continuing insurgency left most of its targets unmet.</p></div>
